"""`aspect setup bazelrc` — write a Bazel rc that configures vanilla `bazel` calls to
use Aspect's remote services and recommended optimizations. `aspect ci bazelrc`,
the name the CI integrations in the wild call, is the same task under its old
group.

`aspect <task>` injects its Bazel flags itself (`feature/workflows.axl`,
`feature/deployment.axl`, and `lib/aspect_flags.axl`, the table of every flag
Aspect sets and why). A bare `bazel build` started from the same job gets none
of that. This command writes the equivalent where vanilla `bazel` will find it.
It is the in-CLI successor to `rosetta bazelrc`: the CI integrations
(the setup-aspect GitHub Action, Buildkite plugin, CircleCI orb, GitLab
component) call it, as `aspect ci bazelrc`, once `rosetta` is gone from the
runner image.

Where the rc goes. One layout under one of two base directories: the generated
rc at `<base>/.aspect/bazelrc`, `try-import`ed from `<base>/.bazelrc`, the rc
Bazel already reads there, and the execution log under
`<base>/.aspect/generated/`. The import line is added once at the top of the
importing rc under a comment, a blank line apart from its other content — at the
top so that file's own lines come after and win where they disagree, which is
how a recommendation is overridden.

  - The checkout is the base by default, and both files are meant to be
    committed, so everyone who builds the repository gets the same rc. The
    generated file is a starting point the repository then owns: edit it freely;
    a rerun of the command restores the generated version. Nothing in it may be
    this machine's: the credential helper is the tool's name, the CI host's
    group is left out, and the execution log is placed through `%workspace%`,
    the one variable Bazel expands in rc values, so the path holds in every
    clone. `.aspect/generated/` is where tools write into a checkout at build
    time; the command creates it with a `.gitignore` of `*` and `!.gitignore`,
    committed so the directory exists in every clone — Bazel does not create the
    log's parent. The workspace root is `ctx.std.env.bazel_root_dir()`, found by
    the boundary files Bazel finds `%workspace%` by, and the command refuses
    where none is.
  - `--home` makes the home directory the base instead, and then the rc is this
    machine's: never committed, rewritten whole on every run, free to name the
    CI host's group and to hold absolute paths, and written without a workspace
    to write into. It is the CI shape — a stock ephemeral runner (GitHub-hosted,
    say) throws the home directory away with the job, and the CI integration
    passing `--home` leaves the checkout untouched. `~/.aspect/generated/` is
    created for the execution log, without the `.gitignore` a checkout gets.
  - An Aspect Workflows runner is always `--home`. The runner is ours and
    nothing else configures Bazel on it, so rewriting the file every job is what
    keeps it in step with the runner's environment; tasks run with `--nohome_rc`
    and stream BES from the CLI, so nothing in it reaches them. Its execution
    log is the exception to the base: the job tmpdir, swept with the job, so
    concurrent jobs on a persistent runner do not share one.

A machine's rc — either home case — also *enables* the tuning that presumes no
endpoint, so a plain `bazel build` on a CI runner picks up `aspect-common` and
the host group without naming a `--config`. Only the endpoints wait, since
choosing a deployment is the caller's.

One thing neither base solves: the execution log path is fixed per base, so two
concurrent builds of one checkout under different `--output_base`s overwrite
each other's log. Bazel expands only `%workspace%` in an rc value — there is no
output-base or invocation-id variable to key it by — so a repository that builds
that way drops the group (`--omit-bazel-flag=--execution_log_compact_file`).

What it holds: every flag sits in a named `--config` group from
`lib/rc_groups.axl`, and where the endpoints come from is what varies. Where the
environment names the deployment — the runner marker, or any `ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_*`
endpoint — the endpoint groups are enabled at the top with
`common --config=<group>` lines too, `--config=aspect-exec` opts into the
runner's executor, and no deployment sections are written: a
`--config=aspect-cloud` in a runner's rc would route a vanilla build off the
runner's own services. Where the environment is silent, the configured
deployments (`aspect auth status`) get the opt-in sections of
`lib/deployment_rc.axl`, each chaining the common and CI-host groups and the
tuning its endpoints justify, so no endpoint is reached until a section is named
— which is what makes a checkout's rc safe to import into a developer's own.
`--config=aspect-cloud` is always among them: the built-in Aspect Cloud entry
states its own endpoints (`engine/aspect/auth.rs`), so the command works on a
machine that has never run `aspect auth login` and the rc it writes starts
working the moment there is a credential.
`--announce_rc` names the group every flag came from either way.
Startup options have no `--config`, so the runner's output paths are plain
`startup` lines. The
credential helper the sections name is `aspect`, which Bazel resolves on PATH
like a shell would; a checkout's rc is shared by everyone who builds it, so it
names the tool, not a path, and the command warns when `aspect` is not on PATH
where it runs. A user who authenticates with a long-lived token instead drops
the helper in their own `~/.bazelrc` and sets `--remote_header`.

Bazel reads the home rc after the workspace rc, so on a runner a repository
whose own rc names a different `--bes_backend` for vanilla calls keeps it by
opting out: `--omit-bazel-flag=--bes_backend`. That knob drops any tuning flag
inside whichever group holds it (a group emptied that way disappears along with
the lines that named it); the endpoints, credentials, and runner paths in
`PROTECTED_FLAGS` are refused. Tasks have their own knob, `--bazel-flags:omit`
(`feature/bazel_flags.axl`), since vanilla `bazel` and tasks may want different
omissions.

The layout above is `rc_paths` in `lib/bazelrc_file.axl`, a pure function of the
base directory and this task's flags; how the rc renders and how the import line
is placed live there too. This file resolves the base — the only part that needs
the filesystem — and writes what comes back. Version-gated flags are resolved against the detected
Bazel version (assumed-latest when undetectable) before writing. Only file
output is supported — there is no stdout mode.
"""

load(
    "./private/lib/aspect_flags.axl",
    "omit_flags",
    "protected_omissions",
    "runner_exec_log_path",
    "runner_startup_flags",
    "unscoped",
)
load(
    "./private/lib/bazelrc_file.axl",
    "GENERATED_HEADER",
    "HOME_HEADER",
    "ImportAction",
    "RUNNER_HEADER",
    "RcConfig",
    "command_sections",
    "ensure_import",
    "import_line",
    "is_config_chain",
    "prune_configs",
    "rc_paths",
    "render_bazelrc",
    "startup_flags_for_rc",
)
load("./private/lib/deployment_rc.axl", "config_collisions", "deployment_configs")
load("./private/lib/environment.axl", "bazel_misdetects_color", "env_names_deployment", "get_workflows_environment", "info", "is_aspect_workflows_runner", "warn")
load("./private/lib/rc_groups.axl", "bes_chains", "rc_groups", "section_chains")

_BAZEL_BOUNDARY_FILES = ["MODULE.bazel", "REPO.bazel", "WORKSPACE.bazel", "WORKSPACE"]

_HELPER_NAME = "aspect"

_IMPORT_VERBS = {
    ImportAction("created"): "created %s with it",
    ImportAction("unchanged"): "%s already imports it",
    ImportAction("prepended"): "added the import at the top of %s",
}

def _home(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """The home directory home mode writes under; ends the task when it can't be
    resolved, since every path of that mode hangs off it."""
    home = ctx.std.env.home_dir()
    if home == None:
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "Could not resolve your home directory, so there is nowhere to put ~/.aspect/bazelrc. Pass --output=<path> and --import-into=<rc> explicitly.")
    return home.rstrip("/")

def _workspace(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """Bazel's workspace root — the directory it substitutes for `%workspace%`
    in an rc `import`, found from the working directory the way Bazel finds it,
    by the nearest boundary file. Ends the task where there is none: with no
    workspace there is no `.bazelrc` for vanilla `bazel` to read."""
    root = ctx.std.env.bazel_root_dir().rstrip("/")
    if not any([ctx.std.fs.exists(root + "/" + marker) for marker in _BAZEL_BOUNDARY_FILES]):
        ctx.std.process.exit(1, "No Bazel workspace above %s (no MODULE.bazel, REPO.bazel, or WORKSPACE), so there is no .bazelrc for vanilla bazel to read. Run this from inside the workspace, or pass --output=<path> and --import-into=<rc>." % ctx.std.env.current_dir())
    return root

def _create_generated_dir(ctx: TaskContext, dir: str, gitignore: bool) -> list:
    """Create `dir`, the execution log's parent, which Bazel will not create
    itself. A checkout's also gets a `.gitignore` of everything but itself:
    committed, that makes the directory exist in every clone while nothing
    written there is ever tracked. A home directory is not a checkout, so there
    it is the bare directory. Returns the files written, for the report."""
    ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(dir)
    if not gitignore:
        return []
    ctx.std.fs.write(dir + "/.gitignore", "*\n!.gitignore\n")
    return [dir + "/.gitignore"]

def _write(ctx: TaskContext, path: str, content: str) -> None:
    """Write `content` to `path`, creating parent directories."""
    parent = path.rstrip("/").rsplit("/", 1)
    if len(parent) == 2 and parent[0] and not ctx.std.fs.exists(parent[0]):
        ctx.std.fs.create_dir_all(parent[0])
    ctx.std.fs.write(path, content)

def _resolve(ctx: TaskContext, flags: list, bazel_version: str | None) -> list:
    """Resolve the version-gated `(flag, constraint)` tuples in `flags` against the
    detected Bazel version via the bazelrc runtime: a blank RunCommand whose
    `common` bucket holds every flag, expanded assumed-latest when the version is
    unknown. Config chain entries are kept verbatim ahead of the rest — the blank
    RunCommand has no section for them to expand into. Command scopes are
    dropped: the rc writes every flag under `common`, which Bazel applies only
    where the option is accepted."""
    chain = [f for f in flags if is_config_chain(f)]
    rest = [unscoped(f) for f in flags if not is_config_chain(f)]
    rc = ctx.bazel.new_rc(flags = rest, version = bazel_version)
    return chain + rc.expand(command = "common")

def _groups(ctx: TaskContext, environment, on_runner: bool, env_deployment: bool, machine_rc: bool, exec_log_path: str) -> (list, list):
    """`rc_groups` for this host: every group defined, and the names enabled."""
    return rc_groups(environment, on_runner, bazel_misdetects_color(ctx.std.env), exec_log_path, env_deployment, machine_rc)

def _credential_helper(ctx: TaskContext) -> str:
    """What the sections hand Bazel as the credential helper: `aspect`, which
    Bazel resolves on PATH the way a shell does. A checkout's rc is read by
    everyone who builds it, so it names the tool, never this machine's path to
    it; where `aspect` is missing from PATH here, say so, since a vanilla build
    on this machine would fail at the helper."""
    if ctx.std.process.which(_HELPER_NAME) == None:
        warn(ctx.std, "`aspect` is not on PATH here. The rc names it as Bazel's credential helper, so put it on PATH before a vanilla `bazel` reads the rc (the CI integrations install it).")
    return _HELPER_NAME

def _sections(ctx: TaskContext, groups: list) -> list:
    """The `--config` sections for this machine's configured deployments, each
    chaining `section_chains(groups)` first and, with a BES, `bes_chains(groups)`.
    Reads `deployments()`, not `list()`: nothing here needs login status."""
    return deployment_configs(ctx.aspect.auth.deployments(), _credential_helper(ctx), section_chains(groups), bes_chains(groups))

def _bazelrc_impl(ctx: TaskContext) -> int | TaskConclusion:
    omitted = ctx.args.omit_bazel_flags
    blocked = protected_omissions(omitted)
    if blocked:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "--omit-bazel-flag cannot drop %s: endpoints, credentials, and the runner's paths are what make the build Aspect's." % ", ".join(blocked))

    if ctx.args.home and ctx.args.output:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "--home and --output both choose where the rc goes. Pass one: --home writes ~/.aspect/bazelrc, --output writes the path you name.")

    environment = get_workflows_environment(ctx.std)
    on_runner = is_aspect_workflows_runner(ctx.std.env)
    home_mode = ctx.args.home or on_runner

    startup_flags = []
    runner_log = ""
    if on_runner:
        if environment.runner == None:
            return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "Running on an Aspect Workflows runner but the runner metadata env vars are missing, so the cache flags cannot be generated. This usually means an older runner image; upgrade the runner.")
        startup_flags = startup_flags_for_rc(runner_startup_flags(environment, ctx.std.env.aspect_root_dir()))

        # Per job rather than per machine, and swept with the job, so concurrent
        # jobs on a persistent runner do not share one log.
        runner_log = runner_exec_log_path(environment)

    # `--output` and `--import-into` together name every path `rc_paths` would
    # otherwise derive, so neither base has to exist to honor them.
    overridden = ctx.args.output and ctx.args.import_into
    base = "" if overridden else (_home(ctx) if home_mode else _workspace(ctx))
    paths = rc_paths(base, output = ctx.args.output, import_into = ctx.args.import_into, home_mode = home_mode, exec_log = runner_log)

    env_deployment = env_names_deployment(ctx.std.env)

    # The rc is this machine's when it is written outside the checkout, and also
    # when the env names the endpoints, which are this machine's either way.
    machine_rc = home_mode or env_deployment
    (groups, enabled) = _groups(ctx, environment, on_runner, env_deployment, machine_rc, paths.exec_log)
    sections = [] if env_deployment else _sections(ctx, groups)

    # A backstop: the built-in Aspect Cloud entry states its own endpoints, so
    # there is always at least one section. Without it an rc naming no endpoint
    # would be a silent no-op, and the report would take `max()` of nothing.
    if not env_deployment and not sections:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "No deployment here advertises an endpoint for the rc to point `bazel` at. Run `aspect auth configure <host>` for the deployment you build against, then rerun `aspect setup bazelrc`.")
    configs = groups + sections
    collisions = config_collisions(configs)
    if collisions:
        return TaskConclusion(exit_code = 1, message = "\n".join(collisions))

    bazel_version = ctx.bazel.version()
    startup_flags = omit_flags(startup_flags, omitted)
    configs = [
        RcConfig(name = c.name, source = c.source, comment = c.comment, flags = _resolve(ctx, omit_flags(c.flags, omitted), bazel_version))
        for c in configs
    ]
    (configs, enabled) = prune_configs(configs, enabled)

    written = []
    if paths.generated_dir:
        written.extend(_create_generated_dir(ctx, paths.generated_dir, gitignore = not home_mode))
    header = RUNNER_HEADER if on_runner else (HOME_HEADER if home_mode else GENERATED_HEADER)
    _write(ctx, paths.output, render_bazelrc(startup_flags, enabled, command_sections(bazel_version), configs, header = header))
    written.insert(0, paths.output)

    import_result = None
    if paths.importer:
        existing = ctx.std.fs.read_to_string(paths.importer) if ctx.std.fs.exists(paths.importer) else None
        import_result = ensure_import(existing, import_line(paths.imported))
        if import_result.action != ImportAction("unchanged"):
            _write(ctx, paths.importer, import_result.content)

    shown = lambda path: _relative(path, base, home_mode)
    if env_deployment:
        optional = [c.name for c in configs if c.name not in enabled]
        summary = "Wrote %s: enabled --config groups %s; opt-in %s (the ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_* environment names this machine's deployment, so no deployment sections)" % (shown(paths.output), ", ".join(enabled), ", ".join(optional) or "none")
        if import_result != None:
            summary += "; " + _IMPORT_VERBS[import_result.action] % shown(paths.importer)
        info(ctx.std, summary + ".")
        return 0

    group_names = [g.name for g in groups]
    sections = [c for c in configs if c.name not in group_names]
    info(ctx.std, "Generating %s: Bazel flags that point plain `bazel` calls at Aspect Cloud and, when configured, Aspect single-tenant deployments." % shown(paths.output))
    for line in _report(written, import_result, paths, shown, sections, enabled, home_mode):
        print(line)
    return 0

def _relative(path: str, base: str, home_mode: bool) -> str:
    """`path` as the summary shows it: relative to `base` when under it —
    `~/`-prefixed in home mode, where a bare `.aspect/bazelrc` would read as the
    checkout's — and absolute otherwise."""
    prefix = base + "/"
    if not base or not path.startswith(prefix):
        return path
    return ("~/" if home_mode else "") + path[len(prefix):]

def _report(written: list, import_result, paths, shown, sections: list, enabled: list, home_mode: bool) -> list:
    """What the command did and how to use the result, printed after the lead
    line: the files written, whether the import was added or already there, and
    whether they belong to the repository or to this machine, then any groups
    already enabled, then the `--config` names the rc defines and what each
    wires, then that Aspect's own tasks reach the default deployment with
    `--remote` and need none of it, last so it is what a developer sees."""
    lines = [""]
    for path in written:
        lines.append("  wrote  " + shown(path))
    if import_result != None:
        if import_result.action == ImportAction("unchanged"):
            lines.append("  kept   %s (already imports %s)" % (shown(paths.importer), paths.imported))
        else:
            lines.append("  added  try-import %s at the top of %s" % (paths.imported, shown(paths.importer)))
    lines.append("These are this machine's, outside the checkout." if home_mode else "Commit these with your repository.")

    if enabled:
        lines.append("")
        lines.append("Applied to every bazel call on this machine: %s." % ", ".join(enabled))

    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Choose a deployment with --config on any bazel command:")
    lines.append("")
    width = max([len(c.name) for c in sections]) + len("--config=")
    for c in sections:
        flag = "--config=" + c.name
        lines.append("  %s%s  %s" % (flag, " " * (width - len(flag)), c.comment))
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("The file is rewritten on every run." if home_mode else "The file is a starting point: edit it as your repository needs; rerunning `aspect setup bazelrc` restores it.")

    lines.append("")
    lines.append("Aspect Workflows tasks need no --config: pass --remote (`aspect build --remote`, `aspect test --remote`) and the default deployment is used automatically.")
    return lines

_BAZELRC_ARGS = {
    "home": args.boolean(
        default = False,
        description = "Write the machine's rc under the home directory — `~/.aspect/bazelrc`, imported by `~/.bazelrc` — instead of the checkout's `.aspect/bazelrc`. For a CI runner whose home directory is thrown away with the job: nothing lands in the checkout, so the rc cannot be committed by accident and needs no workspace to write into. Always on when running on an Aspect Workflows runner. Cannot be combined with --output, which names the path itself.",
    ),
    "output": args.string(
        default = "",
        description = "Rc file to write, rewritten on every run, instead of the default `<base>/.aspect/bazelrc` — the base being the home directory under --home or on an Aspect Workflows runner, and the workspace root elsewhere. Parent directories are created if needed. Only file output is supported — there is no stdout mode.",
    ),
    "import_into": args.string(
        long = "import-into",
        default = "",
        description = "Rc that receives the `try-import` line for the written rc, added at its top only when not already present, so the file's own lines override the import. Defaults to the `.bazelrc` of whichever base the rc was written under: the workspace one, importing `%workspace%/.aspect/bazelrc`, or `~/.bazelrc` under --home and on an Aspect Workflows runner, importing the written path.",
    ),
    "omit_bazel_flags": args.string_list(
        long = "omit-bazel-flag",
        description = "A Bazel flag this rc would otherwise set that you do not want, named as Aspect spells it (e.g. --omit-bazel-flag=--heap_dump_on_oom; a value after `=` is ignored). Repeat for several. Set it once per repo in config.axl via ctx.tasks[\"setup/bazelrc\"].args.omit_bazel_flags. Endpoints, credentials, and the runner's output paths cannot be omitted. Tasks have their own knob, --bazel-flags:omit.",
    ),
}

bazelrc = task(
    group = ["setup"],
    summary = "Write a Bazel rc that points vanilla `bazel` calls at Aspect's remote services and recommended optimizations.",
    description = "Write a Bazel rc so vanilla `bazel` calls pick up the same configuration that `aspect <task>` injects. The rc goes to `<workspace>/.aspect/bazelrc` and the workspace `.bazelrc` gains `try-import %workspace%/.aspect/bazelrc` at its top under a comment, added once, so the repository's own lines override — commit both. With --home, always on an Aspect Workflows runner, the same pair is written under the home directory instead (`~/.aspect/bazelrc`, imported by `~/.bazelrc`): the machine's own rc, rewritten whole on every run and never committed, for a CI runner that is thrown away with the job. Every flag sits in a named `--config` group — `aspect-common`, `aspect-cache`, `aspect-bes`, `aspect-<ci host>`, `aspect-runner`, and the opt-in `aspect-exec` — and a machine's rc enables at the top the groups that apply on this host (`bazel build --announce_rc` shows which group set what). On a Workflows runner, or wherever the `ASPECT_WORKFLOWS_*` environment names an endpoint, that is the recommended CI/profiling flags plus that remote cache, BES, repository cache, and per-runner output paths, with `--config=aspect-exec` opting into its remote executor, and no deployment sections. Where the environment names no endpoint the deployment sections are opt-in, each bringing the common and CI-host tuning with it: `--config=aspect-cloud` for Aspect Cloud's remote cache + BES, `--config=aspect-<name>` for a configured deployment (`aspect auth status`), and `--config=aspect-<name>-exec` to add its remote execution — authenticated through the `aspect get` credential helper. No section names the default deployment, which `aspect auth use` could change under a committed file. `aspect ci bazelrc` is an alias.",
    implementation = _bazelrc_impl,
    args = _BAZELRC_ARGS,
)

ci_bazelrc = task(
    group = ["ci"],
    kind = "bazelrc",
    summary = "Alias of `aspect setup bazelrc`, kept for the CI integrations that call it.",
    description = "The same command as `aspect setup bazelrc`, under the name the setup-aspect GitHub Action, Buildkite plugin, CircleCI orb, and GitLab component call; new callers should use `aspect setup bazelrc`. Config overrides for this name go to ctx.tasks[\"ci/bazelrc\"].",
    implementation = _bazelrc_impl,
    args = _BAZELRC_ARGS,
)
